## N1 — Alternative routes

The actual claims are (A) a fixed nonscalar bias cannot be removed over a positive window merely by endpoint correction followed by passive continuation under that bias, and (B) the particular two-qubit Heisenberg plant has no all-transition one-qubit port after any static bias confined to one designated qubit. Their proofs exhaust the respective defined comparator/bias families. They are not five distinct architecture closures.

| Route | Assessment and honest scope |
|---|---|
| Adjust the endpoint channel while retaining the same biased continuation | ATTEMPTED analytically in source A: the triangle inequality gives a minimax lower bound. Its exact one-qubit counterexample is retained. |
| Change scalar part, direction, amplitude or local B within the Heisenberg single-port bias family | ATTEMPTED analytically and by exact identities in source B: scalar bias preserves degeneracy; every nonscalar direction rotates to the product-eigenvector selection rule. These are parameter cases of one route, not several normalized families. |
| Attenuate an added bias from a supplied strong point | Positive alternative proved conditionally in the transfer source; it escapes A's fixed-coefficient hypothesis. It does not supply a strong point in B's restricted counterexample. |
| Add biases on other old sites or a genuine two-qubit bias | OUTSIDE the quantified B family; not ruled out or executed. Terminal obligation: exhibit a strong point with admissible support and paid reserves. |
| Use a multi-qubit port | OUTSIDE the B hypothesis. The XX transition between the product states is nonzero, so the stated selection rule disappears; a complete all-transition/geometry construction remains unproved here. |
| Use time-dependent controls or an autonomous switch/continuing compensator | OUTSIDE passive continuation A and static-spectrum reduction B. The full Lie certificate supports access to noncommuting mechanisms, but no complete autonomous implementation is derived here. |
| Use a realization not requiring the all-transition bus hypothesis | OPEN alternative; no exclusion from either argument. Terminal obligation is its own complete output, timing, error and incident-cap theorem. |

No retained-authority citation is substituted for these new proofs. The five-closed-family schema is unmet; PASS is not claimed. The bounded task did not authorize closing the broader alternatives.

## N2 — Relations between boundaries

These are two different residuals, not a count of independent walls for one universal no-go. Attenuation can remove A's window defect while failing to produce a strong point for B's Heisenberg plant. Conversely H=0,C=Omega n supplies the strong point but leaves A's fixed-bias window defect. Thus neither fact alone resolves the other's distinct obligation. Geometry, input computability and physical calibration remain hypotheses of the positive transfer, not asserted independent impossibility walls.

## N3 — Hidden conditions

The proofs were reread for hidden restrictions. A requires passive continuation under H+C and vanishing extra holding action; active compensation is explicitly excluded from its conclusion. B requires the exact isotropic two-qubit H, bias supported on the designated first qubit, and a one-qubit B; common SU(2) rotation preserves these supports. The positive transfer requires scalable added blocks, a supplied strong point, vacant geometry, computable input names for its effective version, and the qualified bus's preparation/calibration/isolation data. These conditions are stated explicitly. No convention, audit registration or physical primitive is used to close them.

