## N5 — Resolution audit

* Per element: the forbidden transition is the specific |up up> to |down down> matrix element of any one-qubit B after the stated rotation; no statement about every matrix element is made.
* Per site: all scalar and Bloch-vector biases on one designated qubit are covered analytically; multiple-site biases are not covered.
* Per mode: no continuum or field-mode statement was tested. The four finite energy levels are the complete old spectrum in the counterexample.
* Per block: the complete two-qubit Hamiltonian, all 16 Lie-certificate columns and the selected transition were checked with exact matrices.
* Lattice wide: the old plant in the counterexample is exactly two adjacent qubits. No arbitrary-size lattice obstruction is inferred. The transfer theorem retains its supplied finite plant/geometry hypotheses.

These are written scope statements, not a claim that the required primary canonical runner cache contains an N5 publication certificate. No canonical cache mutation was authorized. That gate obligation remains open.

## N6 — Partial closure and conventions

No new axiom is claimed necessary. Attenuation is an explicit positive route when allowed. A scalar bias is absorbed by the retained comparison phase. A stronger supplied static bias/port may satisfy the positive theorem in another allowed family. A switch or continuing compensator is a separate concrete construction problem. The vanishing local matrix element and drift-channel mismatch are algebraic facts, so a relabeling of reserves or phase conventions cannot change them; changing the allowed operator support or dynamics can. No primitive-registry absence claim is made, so no registry scan is invoked as an impossibility argument.

## N7 — Steelman

The strongest objection to a broad reading is that full local-control Lie accessibility already supplies noncommuting mechanisms unavailable to one static biased eigenbasis. A two-qubit port directly connects the missing product pair, and a bias on another site can break the residual symmetry used in the proof. For the window problem, an autonomous compensator could supply the relative drift after the endpoint rather than allowing free H+C continuation. The terminal obligations are explicit complete-space constructions, with their controller outputs, calibration, timing and incident costs. These are real unclosed alternatives to a universal impossibility claim. They lie outside the two quantified narrow statements and therefore narrow the conclusion without refuting those statements. The narrow conclusions rest on exact proofs, not inability to imagine another route.

